{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-9c15b61b799cbc38", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tA Unifying Perspective on Audio Generative Modeling:\n2\tLatent Representations and Modeling Strategies\n3\t\n4\tarXiv:2608.08667v1 [eess.AS] 9 Aug 2026\n5\t\n6\tDongchao Yang*\n7\tThe Chinese University of Hong Kong\n8\tdcyang@se.cuhk.edu.hk\n9\t\n10\tAbstract\n11\tEvery audio generative system makes two coupled decisions: what representation to generate,\n12\tand how to model its distribution. This paper organizes audio generative modeling around this\n13\tcoupling. For representation design, we compare discrete, continuous, and hybrid latents through\n14\tfour objectives: representation burden, distortion, empirical modelability, and streaming compatibility.\n15\tFor distribution modeling, rather than treating a latent’s difficulty as an intrinsic scalar, we use two\n16\tdiagnostic dimensions: dependency horizon, how far useful context extends, and conditional ambiguity,\n17\thow much uncertainty remains after conditioning. These dimensions refine the common semanticversus-acoustic intuition: variables with a long dependency horizon should receive global modeling\n18\tcapacity. Conditionally ambiguous detail may be delegated to a local or iterative generator. Applied\n19\tto representative systems, this view shows that RVQ’s residual order gives ordered capacity but not\n20\tordered semantics, that AudioLM’s semantic-versus-acoustic cascade is one explicit placement of this\n21\tboundary rather than a universal template, and that autoregression, iterative refinement, and hybrid\n22\tdesigns differ chiefly in how they trade dependency horizon against critical-path generation cost.\n23\tThe distinction between discrete and continuous latents describes the output interface; dependency\n24\thorizon, conditional ambiguity, and streaming determine how that interface should be modeled. Rather\n25\tthan cataloguing individual systems, we provide an evaluation and design framework for comparing\n26\trepresentation–model pairs.\n27\t\n28\t1\n29\t\n30\tIntroduction\n31\t\n32\tEvery audio generative system makes two coupled choices: what latent representation to generate, and\n33\thow to model its distribution. Although these choices are often implemented in separate modules, with\n34\tan encoder or codec followed by a generative model, they cannot be evaluated independently. The latent\n35\tdetermines the sequence length, the information carried at each step, and the dependencies exposed to\n36\tthe generator. The modeling strategy determines how those dependencies are factorized and how the\n37\tremaining uncertainty is represented. Together, they determine generation quality, computational cost,\n38\tand whether the system can operate in real time. We aim to provide a structured perspective on audio\n39\tgenerative modeling, not an exhaustive catalogue or a theorem about one optimal architecture.\n40\tSoundStream [1] and EnCodec [2] illustrate one form of this coupling. They keep the temporal frame\n41\trate low by representing each frame with multiple codebook indices through residual vector quantization\n42\t(RVQ). The resulting latent has two dependency axes, time and codebook depth, and is no longer a\n43\tstandard one-token-per-step LM target. Flattening both axes preserves rich conditioning but increases\n44\t* This is a preliminary version. Comments and corrections are welcome.\n45\t\n46\t1\n47\t\n48\t\fserial generation cost. Predicting codebooks in parallel is faster but removes part of their conditional\n49\tstructure. VALL-E [3], MusicGen [4], UniAudio [5], and Moshi [6] choose different compromises\n50\tbetween these two costs. The codec has therefore not only compressed the waveform; it has also\n51\tdetermined the factorization problem faced by the downstream model. AudioLM [7] illustrates a second\n52\tform of the same coupling: it shows where a representation draws the boundary between global content\n53\tand local, ambiguous detail. Semantic tokens make long-range linguistic structure directly predictable\n54\tbut do not preserve enough information for waveform reconstruction. We interpret AudioLM as an\n55\texplicit allocation: it predicts a semantic trajectory first and generates acoustic tokens conditionally,\n56\tdrawing this boundary between two separate token vocabularies. DiTAR [8] and CALM [9] draw it\n57\tinside a single continuous latent instead. A causal outer process tracks history. Diffusion or a consistency\n58\thead resolves the remaining acoustic ambiguity within each step or block.\n59\tRVQ’s codebook depth and a continuous latent’s per-token dimensionality buy the same representation\n60\tburden in two different ways. AudioLM’s semantic tokenizer and DiTAR’s and CALM’s causal-outer,\n61\tdiffusion-inner split draw the same global-versus-local boundary in two different ways. Discreteness and\n62\tcontinuity decide neither outcome. What varies across these systems is how dependency structure and\n63\tconditional ambiguity are allocated, not the representation’s category.\n64\tThese examples motivate the two questions that organize the paper. What should an audio model\n65\tgenerate? We compare discrete, continuous, and hybrid latents through four requirements: rate,\n66\tdistortion, modelability, and streaming. How should the distribution of that latent be modeled? We\n67\texamine how far its dependencies extend, how much ambiguity remains after conditioning, and whether\n68\tfuture context is available. These properties determine when long-context autoregression, iterative\n69\trefinement, codebook-depth modeling, or a hybrid factorization is appropriate.\n70\tThe analysis proceeds in three steps. We first describe a four-objective representation design space.\n71\tWe then refine the intuition that semantics is low-entropy and acoustics is high-entropy, using two\n72\tdiagnostic questions: which variables benefit from longer context, and which retain multiple acceptable\n73\trealizations after conditioning? Finally, we analyze how different modeling schemes assign these\n74\tvariables to long-context, local, or iterative models. Appendix A provides the codec lineage and technical\n75\tbackground omitted from the main argument.\n76\tContributions.\n77\t• We organize audio generative modeling around the coupled design of an audio latent and the model\n78\tthat learns its distribution.\n79\t• We formulate representation design as a four-objective Pareto problem over burden, distortion,\n80\tempirical modelability, and streaming compatibility.\n81\t• We use dependency horizon, conditional ambiguity, approximation sources, and critical-path\n82\tevaluations as diagnostic tools for comparing autoregressive, codebook-depth, iterative, and hybrid\n83\tsystems, and apply them to representative systems.\n84\t\n85\t2\n86\t\n87\tTwo Decisions Behind Audio Generation\n88\t\n89\tLet x ∈ RL denote a waveform of duration T . An encoder maps it to z = (z1 , . . . , zN ) at frame rate\n90\tr = N/T . For a discrete latent, each zt contains one or more codebook indices; for a continuous latent,\n91\tzt ∈ Rd . We write the encoder and decoder as fθ and gφ , and let pψ model p(z) or p(z | c).\n92\tTwo representation-level quantities can be written directly:\n93\tK\n94\t\n95\t\u0001\n96\tD = Ex ρ x, gφ ( fθ (x)) .\n97\t\n98\tRdisc = r ∑ log2 |Zk |,\n99\tk=1\n100\t\n101\t2\n102\t\n103\t(1)\n104\t\n105\t\fRdisc is the nominal bitrate of a K-codebook discrete representation, and D is perceptual distortion. Ratedistortion theory motivates comparing these two quantities under a source and distortion measure [10].\n106\tA continuous latent has no codec bitrate until a precision or entropy model is specified, so discrete\n107\tand continuous rates should not be collapsed into one scalar. For discrete latents, we report frame\n108\trate, codebook depth, and nominal bitrate; for continuous latents, we report frame rate, dimension, and\n109\tnumerical precision.\n110\tModelability is not a third intrinsic scalar. We report it as an empirical profile indexed by task q,\n111\tdataset D, conditioning c, modeling scheme G, architecture family A, training budget Btrain , and inference\n112\tbudget Binfer :\n113\t\u0001\n114\tMemp ( f ; q, D, c, G, A, Btrain , Binfer ) = Lval , Qgen ,Ctrain ,Cinfer .\n115\t(2)\n116\tHere Lval is a within-objective validation loss, Qgen contains task and perceptual generation measures,\n117\tand Ctrain and Cinfer record realized resource use. Losses from token likelihood, denoising, and flow\n118\tmatching are not compared numerically across objectives. A broad modelability claim should show that\n119\ta ranking persists across several suitable G and A, rather than selecting one architecture that favors a\n120\tlatent.\n121\tStreaming also requires a profile. Encoder lookahead can be defined as\n122\tτenc = inf{ ∆ ≥ 0 : zt depends only on x≤t/r+∆ },\n123\t\n124\t(3)\n125\t\n126\tbut system latency additionally includes decoder lookahead, block duration, critical-path network\n127\tevaluations, computation time, and time to first audio. We therefore use streaming compatibility as a\n128\trepresentation objective and report end-to-end latency for the complete pair.\n129\t\n130\t2.1\n131\t\n132\tA Four-Objective Representation Design Space\n133\t\n134\tThese four quantities define the design space used in the rest of the paper:\n135\t• Representation burden: frame rate and per-frame capacity.\n136\t• Distortion: how much perceptual quality is lost.\n137\t• Empirical modelability: the profile in Equation 2 under declared tasks and budgets.\n138\t• Streaming compatibility: encoder causality and the latency induced when the representation is\n139\tpaired with a generator and decoder.\n140\tBurden\n141\t\n142\tframe rate and capacity\n143\t\n144\tStreaming\n145\t\n146\tcausality and latency\n147\t\n148\tDistortion\n149\t\n150\tperceptual error\n151\t\n152\tModelability\n153\t\n154\ttask and budget indexed\n155\t\n156\tFigure 1: A schematic view of four objectives in audio representation design. The tetrahedral drawing is a\n157\tmnemonic for interacting Pareto objectives, not a metric geometry: the axes have different units and are not\n158\tbarycentric coordinates.\n159\t\n160\t3\n161\t\n162\t\fConcrete design questions couple these objectives: compression versus reconstruction quality; frame\n163\trate versus per-frame modeling burden; reconstruction-optimal versus generator-readable latents; fixedrate versus content-adaptive streaming; and offline quality versus causal generation. The four values do\n164\tnot define a common numerical coordinate system. The figure is a design mnemonic. Empirical results\n165\tshould instead be reported as a Pareto set. Discrete and continuous representations are implementation\n166\tchoices within this space, not competing vertices.\n167\tCompatibility with a pretrained text LLM is not a fifth intrinsic axis. It is modelability relative to a\n168\tparticular model family A. Discrete tokens fit its softmax interface but can dominate context length and\n169\tneed not align with text semantics. LLM-Codec [11] addresses this directly by deriving its codebook\n170\tfrom a pretrained LLM’s token embeddings, so that audio tokens occupy the same vocabulary space the\n171\tlanguage model already uses. Continuous latents require projectors and a generative output head but may\n172\talign more naturally with hidden states. The relevant comparison fixes the shared backbone and compute\n173\tbudget.\n174\t\n175\t2.2\n176\t\n177\tWhen Is a Representation Easier to Model?\n178\t\n179\tWhat does it mean to say that a latent is “easy to model”? It cannot mean that the latent carries an intrinsic\n180\tdifficulty score. Modelability depends on the task, dataset, conditioning, modeling scheme, architecture\n181\tfamily, and both training and inference budgets. The same RVQ stream may be difficult for a flat\n182\ttransformer but easier for a global-over-time, local-over-depth architecture; the same continuous latent\n183\tmay fail under deterministic regression but work under a conditional flow head. A narrow comparison\n184\tshould match every field in Equation 2. A broader claim should evaluate several suitable model families\n185\tand report the resulting Pareto sets.\n186\tIn practice, four measurements expose different failure modes:\n187\t1. Sequence burden: frames or tokens per second, including the effective expansion caused by\n188\tmultiple codebooks.\n189\t2. Predictive burden: held-out loss under the same objective, latent normalization, model family,\n190\tand training budget.\n191\t3. Scaling burden: quality as parameters, context length, training FLOPs, or sampling steps increase.\n192\t4. Task burden: semantic stability, speaker and prosody retention, and perceptual quality in generated\n193\tsamples, not only codec reconstruction.\n194\tNo single one is sufficient. Loss values from different objectives or latent coordinate systems are not\n195\tdirectly comparable. Low token entropy can be manufactured by collapsing useful information. Good\n196\treconstruction can coexist with an irregular latent distribution. Low training loss can reflect an easy but\n197\tsemantically empty target. The meaningful comparison is downstream quality at matched reconstruction\n198\tquality, latency, and compute.\n199\tPrinciple 1 (Reconstruction does not determine modelability). A reconstruction-only codec solves\n200\t(θrec , φrec ) = arg min\n201\tθ ,φ\n202\t\u0002\n203\t\u0001\u0003\n204\tEx ρ x, gφ ( fθ (x)) .\n205\tA representation designed together with its generator instead solves an objective of the form\n206\t\u0002\n207\t\u0001\n208\t\u0001\u0003\n209\t(θ ⋆ , φ ⋆ , ψ ⋆ ) = arg min Ex,c ρ x, gφ ( fθ (x)) + λ Lgen fθ (x); pψ (· | c) .\n210\tθ ,φ ,ψ\n211\t\n212\t4\n213\t\n214\t(4)\n215\t\n216\t(5)\n217\t\n218\t\fThe first objective only requires the latent to work with its decoder. The second also requires it to work\n219\twith the downstream generator. Their optima need not coincide because reconstruction quality does\n220\tnot specify how the latent distribution is organized for a finite generator. The information bottleneck\n221\tprovides a related view: a useful representation should preserve task-relevant information and discard\n222\tirrelevant variability [12].\n223\tDAC [13] documents this gap concretely. Its objective combines adversarial and perceptual losses\n224\twithout a dominant sample-level reconstruction term. Its reconstructions can therefore score poorly\n225\ton waveform-level fidelity measures such as SI-SNR but score strongly on perceptual measures such\n226\tas ViSQOL [14]. The decoder resynthesizes a perceptually equivalent waveform rather than one that\n227\tmatches the reference sample by sample. This is a concrete instance of Principle 1: a distortion measure\n228\tevaluated on samples and one evaluated by a listener or a downstream task need not agree, and a latent\n229\tjudged only by the former has not yet been judged by the criterion a downstream system actually needs.\n230\t\n231\t2.3\n232\t\n233\tHow Should a Latent’s Distribution Be Modeled?\n234\t\n235\tThe representation alone does not decide how its distribution should be modeled. The same latent can\n236\tbe factorized in different orders, or have its remaining uncertainty represented by different generative\n237\tmechanisms. Which choice is appropriate depends on how far its dependencies extend and how much\n238\tambiguity is left after conditioning.\n239\tPrinciple 2 (Predict long-range structure, generate ambiguous detail). For an autoregressive model,\n240\tN\n241\t\n242\tH(z) = ∑ H(zt | z<t ).\n243\t\n244\t(6)\n245\t\n246\tt=1\n247\t\n248\tFor a discrete autoregressive target, the irreducible next-step log loss is the conditional entropy. Let\n249\tst denote structure that may require long-context prediction, and let at denote the remaining acoustic\n250\trealization, so that zt = (st , at ). The chain rule gives\n251\tH(st , at | z<t ) = H(st | z<t ) + H(at | z<t , st ).\n252\t\n253\t(7)\n254\t\n255\tThis decomposition is often summarized as low-entropy semantics followed by high-entropy acoustics.\n256\tThe intuition is useful: content is constrained by linguistic or musical structure. The same content\n257\tcan still admit many perceptually valid acoustic realizations. But the two marginal entropies do not by\n258\tthemselves determine an architecture. Semantic continuation can remain highly uncertain, and speaker\n259\tidentity, prosody, or musical form can give acoustics long-range structure. Our design hypothesis is more\n260\tspecific: st should expose the uncertain variables whose dependencies reward long-context prediction.\n261\tH(at | z<t , st ) measures the remaining realization ambiguity that a conditional acoustic generator must\n262\trepresent. For continuous latents, st and at shape the conditional distribution instead of a token entropy\n263\tthat can be compared numerically.\n264\tThis is the lens we use throughout the paper, but st and at need not be explicit coordinates of a learned\n265\tlatent. They denote functional roles that may remain entangled in practice. We treat dependency horizon\n266\tand conditional ambiguity as diagnostic dimensions, not universal latent statistics.\n267\tA context-ablation curve gives one proxy for dependency horizon. For a fixed representation, task,\n268\tmodel family, and budget, let L (h) be validation loss when the usable history is truncated to duration h,\n269\tand define\n270\thδ = min{h : L (h) ≤ L (hmax ) + δ }.\n271\t(8)\n272\tThe curve, rather than hδ alone, shows whether additional history remains useful. Conditional ambiguity\n273\tis relative to a conditioning set and a perceptual equivalence criterion. Practical proxies include diversity\n274\t5\n275\t\n276\t\famong perceptually acceptable samples at fixed conditioning and the quality gap between a deterministic\n277\tpredictor and a matched distributional head. Neither proxy identifies a unique semantic-versus-acoustic\n278\tsplit, but together they turn the two concepts into testable questions.\n279\t\n280\t3\n281\t\n282\tWhy Audio Representation Design Is Hard\n283\t\n284\tAudio representation design is constrained by three properties of the signal and the generation task.\n285\tLong temporal unrolling. Audio duration translates directly into sequence length. Even after\n286\taggressive downsampling, a 30-second sample can contain hundreds or thousands of latent steps. Frame\n287\trate therefore controls context length, serial generation cost, and the amount of state that a streaming\n288\tmodel must maintain. This is the physical source of the representation burden objective in Section 2.1:\n289\taudio duration forces frame rate to be actively managed rather than a free parameter.\n290\tPerceptual ambiguity. Pointwise waveform agreement is not the same as perceptual agreement.\n291\tTwo signals can remain perceptually equivalent even when phase, noise texture, reverberation, and\n292\tmicro-timing differ. Mel-spectrogram systems exploited this freedom by leaving waveform realization\n293\tto a vocoder, and learned codecs such as DAC [13] can reconstruct natural audio without matching the\n294\twaveform sample by sample. Audio latents should therefore avoid forcing the long-context model to\n295\tresolve every perceptually valid local alternative. This is the physical source of the conditional ambiguity\n296\tterm at in Principle 2: much of what a codec discards is not lost information but detail with more than\n297\tone acceptable realization.\n298\tStructure unfolds over time. Linguistic content, melody, rhythm, speaker state, and prosody evolve\n299\tat different temporal scales. Locally plausible acoustic frames do not guarantee that these trajectories\n300\tremain coherent. A useful latent must keep the long-range variables accessible to the model responsible\n301\tfor planning them. This is the physical source of dependency horizon and the term st in Principle 2: these\n302\tare exactly the variables whose prediction rewards long context.\n303\tThe mel-spectrogram already embodied part of this design before learned codecs. It discarded phase,\n304\tretained a lower-rate time-frequency scaffold, and left waveform realization to a vocoder. Learned codecs\n305\tand continuous audio latents retain the same broad division. They learn what the scaffold should preserve\n306\tinstead of fixing it by design.\n307\tA useful audio latent should keep what the downstream model needs to plan over time, including\n308\tcontent, rhythm, speaker identity, and coarse acoustic structure. It need not expose phase and fine\n309\twaveform texture to the long-context generator when a decoder or local conditional model can realize\n310\tthem.\n311\tThat is the common thread behind mel-spectrograms, semantic tokens, RVQ layers, and continuous\n312\tSSL-based latents. Each one shortens the waveform and reshapes it into something the generator can\n313\tmodel without giving up the information needed to recover natural audio.\n314\t\n315\t4\n316\t\n317\tDiscrete Audio Latents\n318\t\n319\tDiscrete latents turn audio generation into categorical prediction, but discreteness alone does not make\n320\taudio language-like. The downstream burden is determined by four choices: how often a token is emitted,\n321\thow much information each frame carries, whether content is visible in the token stream, and how much\n322\trealization ambiguity remains once that content is fixed. We refer to these as temporal rate, per-frame\n323\tcapacity, dependency horizon, and conditional ambiguity. The first two instantiate representation burden\n324\t(Section 2.1); the last two are Principle 2’s pair of diagnostic dimensions, applied to a discrete latent’s\n325\tcodebook structure.\n326\t\n327\t6\n328\t\n329\t\fFor a K-codebook representation, the relevant question is where the dependencies are placed. Its\n330\tentropy can be expanded along time and codebook depth as\n331\t\u0010\n332\t\u0011\n333\tN K\n334\t<k\n335\tH(z) = ∑ ∑ H ztk | z1:K\n336\t.\n337\t<t , zt\n338\t\n339\t(9)\n340\t\n341\tt=1 k=1\n342\t\n343\tThe equality itself favors no architecture. Under a finite budget, however, the placement matters. Frame\n344\trate determines how many temporal decisions the model must make. Codebook depth determines\n345\thow many conditional decisions remain inside each frame. Dependency horizon determines whether\n346\tlong-range content can be predicted directly or must be inferred through acoustically organized codes.\n347\t\n348\t4.1\n349\t\n350\tTemporal rate and per-frame capacity\n351\t\n352\tA single codebook gives one categorical decision per frame and fits a standard LM interface. At a fixed\n353\tcodebook size, preserving more acoustic detail usually requires a higher frame rate. RVQ takes the other\n354\troute: it keeps the temporal rate low and adds capacity in codebook depth. The first quantizer represents\n355\ta coarse approximation, and later quantizers encode successive residuals.\n356\t\n357\tone token per codebook and frame\n358\ttoken\n359\t\n360\tframe\n361\tvector\n362\t\n363\ttoken\n364\t\n365\ttoken\n366\t\n367\tVQ-1\n368\t\n369\tVQ-2\n370\t\n371\tVQ-3\n372\t\n373\tcoarse residual\n374\t\n375\tresidual\n376\t\n377\tfiner residual\n378\t\n379\teach layer quantizes the residual left by the previous layer\n380\t\n381\tFigure 2: RVQ’s bet: build capacity in depth, not by raising the frame rate. The first quantizer is coarsest; each later\n382\tlayer quantizes the previous residual. One frame can therefore hold enough information for fidelity even at a low\n383\tframe rate.\n384\t\n385\tIf the frame rate is r and there are K codebooks, flattening gives Kr token decisions per second.\n386\tParallel prediction keeps the temporal length at r, but each frame becomes a structured K-variable\n387\tproblem. RVQ therefore does not remove modeling cost. It moves capacity from temporal length into\n388\tdepth, where a downstream architecture may exploit a cheaper local factorization. This is a potential\n389\tmodeling advantage, alongside RVQ’s primary codec benefit of scalable bitrate and fidelity.\n390\tRepresentative systems make the trade-off visible. SoundStream [1] and EnCodec [2] established\n391\tmoderate-frame-rate, multi-codebook RVQ streams. HiFi-Codec [15] and DAC [13] changed the\n392\tquantizer or reconstruction design within the same broad template. Mimi [6] pushes the frame rate to\n393\t12.5 Hz for real-time dialogue by accepting substantial per-frame depth. In contrast, WavTokenizer [16]\n394\tand BigCodec [17] pursue a single-codebook interface at roughly 75–80 Hz. This is not a direct\n395\tleaderboard comparison because training data, bandwidth, causality, and evaluation differ. It nevertheless\n396\texposes the basic exchange: fewer time steps require more information per step.\n397\tThis comparison explains why RVQ remains difficult to replace at the codec layer. SVQ offers\n398\ta simpler interface, but often pays through a higher temporal rate or a larger token space. FSQ-style\n399\t7\n400\t\n401\t\fdesigns [18] improve code utilization, but their scalar dimensions do not automatically provide an\n402\tordered prediction schedule. RVQ provides ordered capacity. It does not provide ordered semantics. The\n403\tsame rate-versus-depth exchange reappears for continuous latents as a rate-versus-dimension exchange\n404\t(Section 5). RVQ’s specific mechanism is not what makes representation burden a real constraint.\n405\tThis capacity-versus-rate trade-off is not resolved by the codec. It is inherited by whatever model\n406\tconsumes the resulting K-codebook stream. A flattened autoregressive model pays the full NK serial\n407\tcost implied by Equation 9 to keep every conditional dependency available. VALL-E [3] instead spends\n408\tserial computation only on the first layer and predicts the remaining K − 1 layers non-autoregressively,\n409\ttrading some cross-layer conditioning for a shorter critical path. MusicGen [4] keeps a causal ordering\n410\tacross codebooks but staggers them with a delay pattern, so several codebooks can be predicted in\n411\tparallel without discarding the ordering entirely. UniAudio [5] and Moshi [6] instead factor the problem\n412\texplicitly: a temporal transformer models frame-to-frame dependence, and a smaller local transformer\n413\tmodels the codebook-depth dependence within each frame. SoundStorm [19] and MAGNeT [20] replace\n414\tthe causal order with iterative masked refinement across the token grid. Each scheme keeps Equation 9’s\n415\tdependencies exact or approximates a specific subset of them. Section 6 and Appendix C compare their\n416\tcritical-path cost directly.\n417\t\n418\t4.2\n419\t\n420\tDependency Horizon in a Discrete Latent\n421\t\n422\tA concrete failure motivates this section. Early autoregressive models trained on SoundStream [1] tokens\n423\talone could preserve locally plausible speech but lose linguistic coherence during continuation: content\n424\tcould drift even though pronunciation remained speech-like. Codec reconstruction could not reveal this\n425\tfailure, since the decoder was given ground-truth tokens. Generation exposed it, because one acoustically\n426\torganized stream asked the long-context model to maintain content and predict local realization detail at\n427\tonce.\n428\tThis is a specific instance of a general requirement. A reconstruction codec only needs its decoder\n429\tto recover audio from z. A generative model needs a stronger property, per Principle 1: task-relevant\n430\tlong-range information must be accessible to the model that predicts z, not only to its decoder. Standard\n431\tRVQ does not guarantee this: its early layers contain whatever most reduces reconstruction error, which\n432\tmay mix phonetic content, pitch, speaker, and channel information.\n433\tAudioLM [7] is the system that exposed this failure, and its fix illustrates one way to guarantee accessibility rather than a template every system must follow. It added semantic tokens from w2v-BERT [21]:\n434\ta semantic LM predicted a low-rate content trajectory, and acoustic LMs generated SoundStream tokens\n435\tconditioned on it. In terms of Principle 2, the system placed long-range content dependence in the\n436\tsequence-level variable s, the aggregate of st across time, and left conditional acoustic variation in\n437\tp(a | s), where a is the corresponding aggregate of at ; Section 4.3 addresses how that second term is\n438\thandled.\n439\t\n440\tw2v-BERT\n441\t\n442\tsemantic tokens\n443\t\n444\tSemantic LM\n445\tlong-range content\n446\t\n447\tconditions\n448\t\n449\tSoundStream\n450\tacoustic RVQ\n451\t\n452\tCoarse acoustic LM\n453\t\n454\tFine acoustic LM\n455\t\n456\tRVQ layers 1-4\n457\t\n458\tremaining RVQ layers\n459\t\n460\tdecoder\n461\twaveform\n462\t\n463\tFigure 3: AudioLM’s cascade: semantic tokens anchor long-range content. Acoustic tokens recover timbre, prosody,\n464\tand detail. The important idea is the division of labor, not the exact three-stage implementation.\n465\t\n466\t8\n467\t\n468\t\fSemantic supervision changes where information appears in Equation 9. SpeechTokenizer [22],\n469\tSoCodec [23], Mimi [6], and SemantiCodec [24] align selected codec components with SSL features\n470\tor semantic targets. X-Codec [25] combines semantic and acoustic encoder features before quantization. HuBERT [26], WavLM [27], or ASR targets can provide the supervisory signal. MOSS-AudioTokenizer [28] instead uses LM-based understanding tasks to strengthen semantic content. These methods\n471\tdo not merely improve a token representation. They try to place content in a low-rate component that a\n472\tlong-context model can read directly. Semantic supervision is one way to satisfy Principle 1, not the only\n473\tconceivable one: the principle only asks that task-relevant information be accessible to the predicting\n474\tmodel, not that an external semantic signal was used to place it there. Whether a reconstruction-only\n475\tdiscrete latent can reach comparable accessibility through favorable geometry or training dynamics alone\n476\tremains an open question, addressed further in Section 7.\n477\tThis need is strongest when the model must continue without a complete external content plan. In\n478\tTTS, text already constrains content, so acoustic-token-only systems can work. Even there, predicting a\n479\tcompact semantic or phonetic plan first usually simplifies the acoustic mapping. The difference is one of\n480\tdegree, not a binary distinction between continuation and TTS.\n481\tAudioLM also makes an assumption that should remain visible: once semantics is known, most\n482\tremaining acoustic dependence is local enough for a cheaper model. Speaker identity, expressive prosody,\n483\troom acoustics, and musical form can violate that assumption. The useful principle is therefore not\n484\t“semantics first” in every system. It is to expose each long-range factor before asking a local generator to\n485\tfill in detail.\n486\tDependency horizon must be balanced with reconstruction and rate under a finite latent capacity.\n487\tAdding semantic supervision is useful only if it reduces downstream modeling difficulty at matched\n488\tfidelity and latency. This is why generator-aware objectives, as in ALMTokenizer [29], are conceptually\n489\timportant: they optimize the interface with the downstream model rather than treating reconstruction as\n490\tthe final criterion. Continuous latents face the same accessibility problem without a codebook layer to\n491\tsupervise; Section 5 addresses how they shape a comparably accessible geometry instead.\n492\tModern systems preserve this division in three ways. Layer assignment places semantic supervision\n493\tin selected codec layers, as in SpeechTokenizer [22] and Mimi [6]. Factor assignment separates timeinvariant information in TiCodec [30], sources in SD-Codec [31], or content, prosody, speaker, and\n494\tresidual variables in FACodec [32]. Model assignment predicts semantic or phonetic variables first and\n495\tuses a conditional acoustic generator afterward, as in CosyVoice [33] and Seed-TTS [34]. The first two\n496\treshape the representation itself. The third leaves the representation alone and reallocates the work to the\n497\tmodeling side instead, a preview of the coupling explored in Section 6.\n498\t\n499\t4.3\n500\t\n501\tConditional Ambiguity in a Discrete Latent\n502\t\n503\tExposing the long-range content s does not remove the conditional acoustic detail a left once s is known.\n504\tRVQ’s later codebook layers still carry pitch micro-detail, timbre, phase-related texture, and other\n505\trealization variables for which more than one value is perceptually acceptable given the same content.\n506\tThis is the discrete-latent instance of conditional ambiguity in Principle 2.\n507\tCategorical prediction has a property continuous regression lacks: a softmax over a codebook is\n508\talready a distribution, and sampling from it already represents more than one acceptable realization.\n509\tUnlike a continuous latent, where a deterministic regressor collapses ambiguity toward a conditional\n510\tmean and a distributional mechanism must be added deliberately (Section 5.3), a discrete latent gets a\n511\tbasic notion of conditional variation for free from its output layer.\n512\tThis does not close the question, because Equation 9’s chain rule still specifies exactly which variables\n513\teach layer may condition on, and no practical model implements it exactly. VALL-E [3] predicts later\n514\tlayers non-autoregressively, approximately treating them as conditionally independent given the first\n515\tlayer. This is fast, but it can discard correlations between codebooks that a truly joint sample would\n516\t\n517\t9\n518\t\n519\t\fpreserve. SoundStorm [19] and MAGNeT [20] instead resolve the remaining tokens through iterative\n520\tmasked refinement, revisiting the same positions multiple times so that later predictions can depend\n521\ton tokens filled in earlier in the schedule rather than committing to full conditional independence in\n522\tone pass. AudioLM’s [7] coarse-to-fine acoustic LMs are a third compromise: each stage conditions\n523\ton the previous stage’s output, narrowing the realization progressively instead of resolving it in one\n524\tnon-autoregressive step or many masked rounds.\n525\tNone of these schemes is uniquely correct. They trade the same two costs already introduced in\n526\tSection 4.1: how much of Equation 9’s conditional structure is preserved, and how many sequential\n527\tnetwork evaluations that preservation costs. Section 6 and Appendix C compare these schedules directly\n528\ton that basis.\n529\tTaken together, Sections 4.1–4.3 point to three levers the literature has used to shape a discrete\n530\tlatent’s modelability, none of them guaranteed to help outside the setting in which it was demonstrated.\n531\t• Representation burden can be moved between frame rate and codebook depth (RVQ) or concentrated into a single larger vocabulary (SVQ, FSQ), changing how many temporal versus conditional\n532\tdecisions a downstream model faces.\n533\t• Dependency horizon can be made accessible by distilling SSL or ASR targets into selected codec\n534\tcomponents, by training the tokenizer against a generator-aware objective instead of reconstruction\n535\talone, or by explicitly factoring content, prosody, and speaker into separate variables.\n536\t• Conditional ambiguity, once content is exposed, is preserved to a degree that depends on whether\n537\tthe model approximates Equation 9’s chain rule by conditional independence, iterative masked\n538\trefinement, or a staged cascade.\n539\tNone of these choices is free: each trades reconstruction, rate, or critical-path cost for the property it\n540\timproves, which is why Section 7 asks for controlled comparisons rather than treating any one of them\n541\tas a default recipe.\n542\t\n543\t5\n544\t\n545\tContinuous Audio Latents\n546\t\n547\tContinuous latents remove categorical quantization, but they do not remove the representation problem.\n548\tTheir downstream burden is controlled by the same four choices as a discrete latent: temporal rate, pertoken dimension, dependency horizon, and conditional ambiguity. The first two instantiate representation\n549\tburden (Section 2.1). The last two are Principle 2’s diagnostic dimensions, applied to a continuous\n550\tlatent’s geometry instead of its codebook structure.\n551\tLet y denote structure that the downstream system must preserve, such as linguistic content, speaker\n552\tidentity, or musical form. Two information terms clarify the tension. I(z; y) measures how much of that\n553\tstructure the latent retains. I(z; x | y) measures how much additional acoustic variation it carries once that\n554\tstructure is known. y plays the role of dependency horizon’s st from Principle 2 for a continuous latent,\n555\tand I(z; x | y) is its conditional ambiguity term at , expressed as retained information rather than token\n556\tentropy. Retention does not guarantee accessibility to a finite model, so semantic probes or matched\n557\tdownstream losses are still needed. The second term is not simply waste: some of it is required for\n558\tfidelity. But if a high-dimensional latent exposes every local variation to the generator, the downstream\n559\tmodel must fit that variation even when it is perceptually ambiguous. Continuous latent design decides\n560\thow much of this conditional detail stays in z and how much is left to the decoder.\n561\t\n562\t5.1\n563\t\n564\tTemporal rate and per-token dimension\n565\t\n566\tA continuous latent trades frame rate against per-token dimension the same way a discrete latent\n567\ttrades frame rate against codebook depth: more information per step allows a lower rate, and a lower\n568\t10\n569\t\n570\t\frate keeps the downstream sequence short. Stable Audio [35]’s VAE, in one released configuration,\n571\tcompresses 44.1 kHz stereo audio by a factor of 2048, giving a 64-dimensional latent at roughly 21.5 Hz.\n572\tNaturalSpeech 2 [36] instead keeps a much higher frame rate, close to 80 Hz, with a single continuous\n573\tvector per frame rather than a multi-codebook stream. CALM [9] uses a 128-dimensional audio-VAE\n574\tframe in at least one open implementation. These are not a leaderboard comparison, since the encoders,\n575\tdomains, and training data differ. They show the same exchange as Section 4.1’s RVQ comparison,\n576\texpressed in dimension rather than codebook depth. Fidelity can be bought with a higher frame rate\n577\tand low per-token dimension, or with a lower frame rate and high per-token dimension. The choice\n578\tdetermines how many autoregressive or diffusion steps the downstream model must take, not only how\n579\tthe encoder compresses audio.\n580\t\n581\t5.2\n582\t\n583\tDependency Horizon in a Continuous Latent\n584\t\n585\tMel-spectrograms and VAE latents expose a compact acoustic scaffold and leave waveform detail to a\n586\tdecoder. Make-An-Audio [37], NaturalSpeech 2 [36], and Stable Audio [35] demonstrate this route across\n587\tgeneral audio, speech, singing, and music. Such latents are efficient generation targets, but reconstruction\n588\ttraining does not require their coordinates to expose content. SSL features take the opposite starting\n589\tpoint. They make phonetic or semantic structure accessible, but are often high-dimensional and retain\n590\tvariations that a generator must reproduce at every frame.\n591\tRecent work tries to build a compressed semantic-acoustic latent between these endpoints. DashengTokenizer [38] starts from frozen semantic features and injects acoustic information. WavCube [39]\n592\tcompresses an SSL representation before acoustic reconstruction and retains a semantic anchor throughout. MingTok-Audio [40] and LoSATok [41] likewise seek representations shared by understanding and\n593\tgeneration.\n594\tIn information terms, semantic alignment aims to preserve information about y in a form accessible to\n595\tthe selected model. Bottlenecking limits the conditional detail exposed to the generator. Reconstruction\n596\tprevents the latent from discarding information required by the decoder. These operations serve different\n597\tpurposes. A high-dimensional semantic latent is not automatically modelable, and a low-dimensional\n598\tlatent is not automatically well organized.\n599\t\n600\t5.3\n601\t\n602\tConditional Ambiguity in a Continuous Latent\n603\t\n604\tContinuous dimension is not a bitrate, but it directly affects the output problem. The failure mode is not\n605\thypothetical: early neural TTS systems that predicted mel-spectrograms under a deterministic L1 or L2\n606\tloss produced characteristically over-smoothed, muffled spectral detail, because squared error averaged\n607\tover every acoustically valid realization of a given phoneme and prosody collapses toward their mean\n608\trather than committing to any one of them. A deterministic regressor must choose one point in latent\n609\tspace. When several acoustic realizations are valid, squared error moves the prediction toward their\n610\tconditional mean. Diffusion, flow matching [42], mixture heads, and other distributional objectives are\n611\tuseful because they can represent this conditional variation. Voicebox [43] is an influential example of\n612\tusing flow matching for conditional speech infilling and generation.\n613\tThese mechanisms are not interchangeable, and audio’s own modeling literature has not worked out\n614\ttheir trade-offs as thoroughly as image generation has. GIVT [44] replaces the categorical output of a\n615\tdiscrete-token transformer with a Gaussian mixture head, predicting a full per-token distribution in one\n616\tforward pass but limiting expressivity to the chosen mixture family. MAR [45] instead attaches a small\n617\tdiffusion head to each token, trading a fixed per-token sampling cost of several denoising steps for a\n618\tdistribution that is not constrained to a parametric family. CALM [9] takes a related route for audio,\n619\tdistilling a consistency model so that most of diffusion’s per-step sampling cost is paid once, offline,\n620\trather than at every generation step. The choice is the same trade-off already introduced for discrete\n621\t\n622\t11\n623\t\n624\t\flatents in Section 4.3: how much of the true conditional distribution a mechanism can represent, against\n625\thow many network evaluations it costs to sample from it.\n626\tContinuous geometry matters even when reconstruction does not change. For any invertible transform\n627\tT , define z′ = T (z) and g′φ (z′ ) = gφ (T −1 (z′ )). The transformed pair preserves the same reconstruction,\n628\tg′φ (z′ ) = gφ (z),\n629\t\n630\t(10)\n631\t\n632\tbut it presents a different density geometry to a finite downstream model. Reconstruction therefore\n633\tcannot select among all equivalent coordinate systems. This is Principle 1 applied to continuous geometry\n634\tspecifically: two encoder-decoder pairs can satisfy the same reconstruction objective but present entirely\n635\tdifferent learning problems to a finite generator. If semantic and nuisance variation are entangled across\n636\tmany dimensions, reducing frame rate alone may still leave a difficult per-step distribution. Continuous\n637\tlatent design must be evaluated together with the downstream loss and model family, just as RVQ must\n638\tbe evaluated together with its depth factorization.\n639\tA convergent observation appears in continuous-token autoregressive image generation. Constraining\n640\tVAE latents to a fixed-radius hypersphere removes a scale degree of freedom that otherwise drives\n641\tvariance collapse under classifier-free guidance. This geometry change alone lets a purely autoregressive\n642\tmodel match or exceed diffusion and masked-generation baselines at comparable scale [46]. The\n643\treconstruction target is unchanged; only the coordinate geometry presented to the autoregressive model\n644\tis.\n645\tHigh-dimensional SSL features often expose long-range structure well but create a large per-frame\n646\tprediction problem. Low-dimensional VAE latents reduce that burden but may hide the same structure.\n647\tCompressed semantic latents try to preserve accessibility and still control dimension and conditional\n648\tacoustic variation. They remain subject to the four-objective design space: compression can increase\n649\tdistortion, semantic alignment can raise representation burden, and non-causal encoders can improve\n650\tempirical modelability at the expense of streaming compatibility.\n651\tTaken together, Sections 5.1–5.3 point to a comparable set of levers for a continuous latent’s\n652\tmodelability, none of them guaranteed to help outside the setting in which it was demonstrated.\n653\t• Representation burden can be moved between frame rate and per-token dimension (Section 5.1),\n654\tthe continuous analogue of RVQ’s rate-versus-depth exchange.\n655\t• Dependency horizon is harder to guarantee than in a discrete latent, because there is no codebook\n656\tlayer to supervise directly. Recent tokenizers instead start from a frozen or lightly adapted semantic\n657\tencoder and inject acoustic detail afterward, rather than the reverse: DashengTokenizer [38] freezes\n658\ta pretrained semantic encoder and adds acoustic information through a single linear projection\n659\tfrom mel-spectrogram features; WavCube [39] first trains a semantic bottleneck to remove the\n660\toff-manifold redundancy that makes raw SSL features difficult to generate from, then injects\n661\tfine-grained acoustic detail under a semantic-anchoring loss that keeps the representation from\n662\tdrifting off that manifold; LoSATok [41] compresses a high-dimensional semantic encoder’s\n663\tfeatures into a much lower-dimensional space under dual-level semantic supervision. This trades\n664\tdimension for accessibility but keeps enough capacity for acoustic detail.\n665\t• Conditional ambiguity, once content is exposed, is preserved to a degree that depends on the\n666\tsame choice as in a discrete latent, but expressed through a distributional output head instead of a\n667\tchain rule: a Gaussian mixture, a diffusion or consistency process, or a deterministic regressor that\n668\tdiscards it entirely.\n669\tNone of these choices is free: compressing toward a semantic encoder can raise distortion or discard\n670\tacoustic detail the decoder still needs, which is why Section 7 again asks for controlled comparisons\n671\trather than a default recipe.\n672\tHow these latents are generated is a separate question, and we return to it in the next section.\n673\t12\n674\t\n675\t\f6\n676\t\n677\tHow to Model an Audio Latent\n678\t\n679\tChoosing a latent defines the random variables and exposes a particular dependency structure. The\n680\tmodeling architecture must decide which dependencies receive causal context, which are handled locally,\n681\tand which variables can be refined jointly. For a K-stream representation, let π be an ordering of its NK\n682\tvariables. A sequential model can use the exact chain-rule factorization\n683\tNK\n684\t\u0001\n685\tp(z | c) = ∏ p zπi | zπ<i , c .\n686\t\n687\t(11)\n688\t\n689\ti=1\n690\t\n691\tPractical architectures choose the order π, restrict the available context, or predict groups of variables\n692\ttogether. Iterative methods make a different choice. Masked models, following the broader maskedgeneration idea exemplified by MaskGIT [47], learn repeated conditional refinement. Diffusion [48] and\n693\tflow matching [42] instead learn a corruption reversal or transport process over groups of variables. They\n694\tare modeling schemes, but not alternative left-to-right chain-rule orderings.\n695\tTheir practical difference appears under finite data, capacity, optimization, and generation time.\n696\tWithin one declared training objective, a conceptual decomposition is\n697\tLval = LG⋆ + εfact + εapprox + εest + εopt ,\n698\t\n699\t(12)\n700\t\n701\twhere LG⋆ is the irreducible risk for scheme G, and the four residuals denote restrictions from the\n702\tchosen factorization or context, finite architecture, finite data, and imperfect optimization. Finite-step\n703\tsamplers add inference truncation error that does not appear in training loss. This decomposition is\n704\tdiagnostic rather than identifiable: ordinary experiments cannot estimate its terms separately. Exact\n705\tdiscrete likelihood has LG⋆ = H(z | c). Denoising and flow objectives instead define different risks and\n706\tmust not be compared numerically with token entropy.\n707\tLet EG denote critical-path network evaluations: the network calls that must occur sequentially before\n708\tan output segment is available. EG is more precise than token count but is still not latency. It must be\n709\treported with FLOPs, hardware, real-time factor, and time to first audio. A practical scheme must control\n710\tvalidation and generation quality under these realized costs.\n711\tThree diagnostic dimensions guide this choice. Dependency horizon asks how model performance\n712\tchanges as usable context grows. Conditional ambiguity asks whether multiple perceptually acceptable\n713\trealizations remain under a declared conditioning set and equivalence criterion. These questions refine\n714\tthe low-entropy-versus-high-entropy intuition in Principle 2. They ask not only how much uncertainty\n715\tremains but also how it is structured. Streaming requirement asks whether future context is available and\n716\thow much end-to-end latency is allowed. Together, these dimensions suggest which modeling strategies\n717\tshould be tested. They do not determine one architecture from the latent alone.\n718\t\n719\t6.1\n720\t\n721\tLong-range causal dependence favors autoregression\n722\t\n723\tAutoregression gives each step access to the generated past. It is valuable when causal history contains\n724\tinformation that a parallel predictor cannot recover from conditioning alone. GSLM [49] and TWIST [50]\n725\tmodel long-range spoken content with semantic units. MusicLM [51] uses semantic and acoustic\n726\thierarchies for music. Dialogue state and persistent speaker or prosodic state can have the same\n727\tlong dependency horizon. A causal AR factorization is compatible with streaming when the encoder,\n728\tconditioning path, decoder, and computation are also causal and fast enough. Tokenwise AR has EG on\n729\tthe order of N. Block prediction can shorten this path.\n730\tThis explains why low-rate semantic tokens are natural AR targets: each serial step advances a longrange state. Applying the same factorization to a high-rate acoustic stream spends serial computation on\n731\tphase, texture, and other locally ambiguous details. The issue is not that AR cannot model acoustics. It\n732\tis whether causal history reduces enough uncertainty to justify the serial path.\n733\t13\n734\t\n735\t\fRVQ adds a second dependency horizon across codebook depth. Flattening time and depth gives\n736\trich context but can raise the critical path toward NK evaluations. Section 4.1 already introduced how\n737\tVALL-E [3], MusicGen [4], and Moshi [6] trade retained conditioning against critical-path cost when\n738\tthey resolve this second horizon. The point that matters here is that the trade-off is a property of the\n739\tdependency structure itself, not of any one system’s specific schedule.\n740\t\n741\t6.2\n742\t\n743\tStrong global conditioning favors iterative refinement\n744\t\n745\tWhen text, duration, or another condition strongly constrains global structure, bidirectional refinement\n746\tcan shorten the critical path without discarding much useful context. SoundStorm [19] and MAGNeT [20]\n747\trepeatedly fill or revise masked codec tokens. DiffSound [52] and InstructTTS [53] use discrete diffusion\n748\tto refine VQ acoustic tokens. Continuous diffusion and flow matching learn different mathematical\n749\tobjects, but make the same systems-level exchange: they update many time positions in parallel over\n750\tseveral network evaluations.\n751\tThis strategy is well matched to strongly conditioned offline TTS or text-to-audio generation. E2TTS [54] and F5-TTS [55] illustrate how full conditioning and non-causal generation can support flowbased sequence synthesis. It is less natural when the system must emit audio before future conditioning\n752\tor future latent positions are available. Iterative refinement is therefore selected by available context, not\n753\tby whether the latent is discrete or continuous.\n754\t\n755\t6.3\n756\t\n757\tHybrids: sequential outside, iterative inside\n758\t\n759\tMany audio latents contain both long-range causal state and locally ambiguous realization. A hybrid\n760\tfactorization assigns them to different scales. Continuous autoregression keeps a causal outer model over\n761\ttime and uses a distributional head for the next frame or block. MELLE [56], DiTAR [8], VibeVoice [57],\n762\tand CALM [9] explore versions of this pattern. Block-causal diffusion and flow are serial across blocks\n763\tand iterative within each block.\n764\tThe design variables are block size and inner sampling cost. In a streaming system, larger blocks\n765\treduce outer critical-path evaluations but add lookahead and make each conditional distribution harder.\n766\tMore inner refinement steps can reduce local approximation error but increase latency. Calling the whole\n767\tsystem AR, diffusion, or flow hides this allocation.\n768\t\n769\t6.4\n770\t\n771\tRepresentative latent-model pairings\n772\t\n773\tAs in Section 4.2, let s denote exposed long-range structure and a the remaining acoustic realization. The\n774\tchain rule gives\n775\tp(s, a) = p(s) p(a | s).\n776\t(13)\n777\tThe value of this order is not that semantics is always low entropy. A compact s shortens the expensive\n778\tserial path and still exposes long-range constraints. Conditioning on s then reduces the dependency\n779\thorizon of much of a, allowing a local or iterative acoustic model to absorb the remaining ambiguity.\n780\tAudioLM [7] places the split between semantic and acoustic tokenizers. CosyVoice [33] places it\n781\tbetween a semantic-token LM and a continuous flow decoder. Mimi within Moshi [6] distills semantic\n782\tinformation into a causal codec layer. Moshi separates temporal prediction from depth prediction.\n783\tVALL-E [3] follows a related but weaker allocation: its first RVQ layer receives temporal AR and later\n784\tlayers receive non-autoregressive refinement, without assuming that the residual order is a semantic\n785\thierarchy. These systems use long-context capacity for the variables they expose as long-range and\n786\tcheaper conditional models where the remaining dependence is shorter or more ambiguous.\n787\tThis view also prevents a category error. Replacing RVQ with one continuous latent does not remove\n788\tthe semantic-acoustic allocation problem. Replacing a flow decoder with discrete tokens does not make\n789\t14\n790\t\n791\t\fcontinuation coherent. Discreteness and continuity define the output interface; dependency horizon,\n792\tconditional ambiguity, and streaming determine how that interface should be modeled.\n793\tTable 1 applies the same questions to seven representative systems. This is a worked literature\n794\tanalysis, not experimental validation: the entries state which variables are explicitly exposed, where\n795\tglobal context is allocated, and what remains for conditional generation.\n796\tTable 1: Worked comparison of representative representation-model pairs. “Global signal” means information\n797\texplicitly available to the component with the longest context; it does not imply a universal semantic ontology.\n798\tSystem\n799\t\n800\tRepresentation and global signal\n801\t\n802\tModeling allocation\n803\t\n804\tCritical path\n805\t\n806\tAudioLM [7]\n807\t\n808\tw2v-BERT semantic tokens plus\n809\tSoundStream RVQ\n810\t\n811\tSemantic LM predicts a low-rate\n812\tcontent trajectory first; acoustic\n813\tLMs realize it conditionally.\n814\t\n815\ttemporal generation at successive\n816\tstages\n817\t\n818\tVALL-E [3]\n819\t\n820\tEnCodec RVQ; text and prompt\n821\tcondition layer 1\n822\t\n823\tFirst codec layer gets temporal\n824\tAR; residual layers get\n825\tnon-autoregressive refinement.\n826\t\n827\tN causal steps for RVQ-1, then\n828\tK − 1 layer-wise refinement\n829\tpasses\n830\t\n831\tMusicGen [4]\n832\t\n833\tEnCodec RVQ with text\n834\tconditioning\n835\t\n836\tDelayed AR prediction preserves\n837\ta causal inter-codebook order\n838\twithout flattening all NK tokens.\n839\t\n840\tapproximately N + K − 1\n841\tpositions\n842\t\n843\tUniAudio [5]\n844\t\n845\tmulti-codebook codec tokens\n846\twith task conditioning\n847\t\n848\tA global temporal transformer\n849\tmodels frame history; a local\n850\ttransformer resolves\n851\twithin-frame codebook depth.\n852\t\n853\ttemporal steps plus local depth\n854\tprediction\n855\t\n856\tSoundStorm [19]\n857\t\n858\tsemantic conditioning plus\n859\tSoundStream tokens\n860\t\n861\tSemantic conditioning plus\n862\tbidirectional masked refinement\n863\tparallelizes codec-token\n864\tgeneration.\n865\t\n866\tscheduled parallel refinement\n867\trounds\n868\t\n869\tDiTAR [8]\n870\t\n871\tcontinuous acoustic frames\n872\tconditioned by text and history\n873\t\n874\tA causal patch-level LM sets\n875\tcontext; a diffusion transformer\n876\tresolves each patch’s detail.\n877\t\n878\tcausal patch steps, each with\n879\tdiffusion evaluations\n880\t\n881\tCALM [9]\n882\t\n883\tcontinuous audio-VAE frames\n884\t\n885\tA causal Transformer sets\n886\tcontext; a consistency head\n887\treplaces categorical prediction\n888\twith continuous sampling.\n889\t\n890\tcausal frame steps, each with\n891\tconsistency-model evaluations\n892\t\n893\tThe table supports three limited conclusions. First, whether the interface is discrete or continuous\n894\tdoes not determine the modeling scheme. Second, global information can be supplied by a latent\n895\tstream, external conditioning, or causal history. Third, reducing critical-path evaluations changes which\n896\tconditional dependencies a finite model must approximate. The table does not establish that one allocation\n897\tis optimal; that requires the controlled comparisons proposed in Section 7.\n898\t\n899\t6.5\n900\t\n901\tWhat should a unified audio model share?\n902\t\n903\tThe preceding discussion uses speech because linguistic content makes long-range structure easy to\n904\tname. A general audio model, however, may cover speech, music, environmental sound, and mixtures\n905\tof them, as pursued by UniAudio [5] and Ming-UniAudio [40]. Unification should not require these\n906\tdomains to share one fixed notion of semantics. It should provide a common interface for variables that\n907\tplay the same modeling role.\n908\tLet g denote variables whose dependency horizon requires global modeling, and let ℓ denote the\n909\tremaining realization detail: the same distinction as Principle 2’s st and at , renamed here because\n910\t“semantic” and “acoustic” are speech-specific labels that do not generalize cleanly across domains. In\n911\tspeech, g may include linguistic content, dialogue state, speaker identity, and discourse-level prosody. In\n912\t15\n913\t\n914\t\fmusic, it may include melody, harmony, rhythm, instrumentation, and section structure. In environmental\n915\tsound, it may describe event order, source persistence, and scene evolution. Singing, audio-visual\n916\tgeneration, and other mixed settings can expose several of these variables at once. What these variables\n917\tshare is not an ontology. An error in g changes the global continuation, whereas multiple values of ℓ may\n918\tremain perceptually valid after g and the conditioning are fixed.\n919\tTable 2: The same functional division appears across audio domains, but the variables occupying each role change\n920\twith the domain and available conditioning.\n921\tDomain\n922\t\n923\tVariables that may require long-context\n924\tmodeling\n925\t\n926\tConditionally ambiguous realization detail\n927\t\n928\tSpeech\n929\t\n930\tlinguistic content, dialogue state, speaker,\n931\tglobal prosody\n932\tmelody, harmony, rhythm, form,\n933\tinstrumentation\n934\tevent sequence, source persistence, scene\n935\tstate\n936\t\n937\texcitation, phase, fricative texture, local\n938\troom response\n939\tmicro-timing, performance variation, local\n940\ttimbral texture\n941\texact event waveform, background texture,\n942\treverberation\n943\t\n944\tMusic\n945\tEnvironmental sound\n946\t\n947\tExternal conditioning can move the boundary. Text supplies much of the linguistic plan in TTS.\n948\tLyrics and symbolic controls can expose part of a musical plan. Video can constrain event timing and\n949\tscene continuity. Without such conditioning, the audio model must generate those variables itself. The\n950\tappropriate split between g and ℓ is therefore conditional on both the domain and the information already\n951\tprovided.\n952\tWe propose the following design hypothesis for unified audio systems. An architecture can share an\n953\tencoder or tokenizer, temporal backbone, conditioning interface, and acoustic decoder without forcing\n954\tevery example into speech-style semantic tokens. Domain prompts, latent factors, specialized heads, or\n955\texperts may expose different global variables and still retain a common generation mechanism. Variables\n956\tthat benefit from longer context should receive global modeling capacity. Detail that remains ambiguous\n957\tunder the available conditioning may be assigned to a local or iterative generator. Whether the same\n958\tallocation works across speech, music, and environmental sound remains an empirical question.\n959\t\n960\t7\n961\t\n962\tOpen Problems in Audio Generative Modeling\n963\t\n964\tThe framework suggests a basic rule for future work: evaluate a representation and its downstream\n965\tmodel as a pair. Existing codec surveys [14] and benchmarks such as DASB [58] provide important\n966\treconstruction and downstream evidence, but the pairing question additionally requires a controlled\n967\tgenerator and inference budget. A new latent should not be judged only by reconstruction, and a new\n968\tgenerator should not be compared across unmatched latent rates or distortion levels. The useful question\n969\tis whether the pair improves generation at matched perceptual quality, training compute, inference cost,\n970\tand lookahead.\n971\t\n972\t7.1\n973\t\n974\tEvaluate modelability under controlled conditions\n975\t\n976\tModelability is currently inferred from downstream results obtained with different architectures and\n977\tbudgets. A controlled study should vary one side of the pair at a time. With the latent fixed, compare\n978\tcompatible temporal, depth, iterative, and hybrid schemes under matched compute and report downstream\n979\tquality rather than raw losses across objectives. Within a representation family, hold the backbone\n980\t\n981\t16\n982\t\n983\t\ffixed and vary frame rate, codebook depth, semantic supervision, or continuous dimension at matched\n984\treconstruction quality.\n985\tThe experiment cannot observe the components of Equation 12 separately, but it can measure their\n986\tpractical consequence under a fixed budget. Predictive or denoising loss should therefore be accompanied\n987\tby semantic stability, speaker and prosody retention, perceptual quality, critical-path evaluations, and\n988\treal-time cost. Reconstruction and generation must be reported separately. Short-form TTS is not a\n989\tsubstitute for continuation, and offline throughput is not a substitute for streaming latency.\n990\t\n991\t7.2\n992\t\n993\tCo-design the latent and its modeling strategy\n994\t\n995\tMost systems optimize a codec for reconstruction, freeze it, and train the generator afterward. This\n996\tis convenient, but it leaves the downstream interface out of codec design. Co-design should decide\n997\twhether capacity is placed in time, codebook depth, or feature dimension, and whether the downstream\n998\tarchitecture provides the context and output distribution needed at that location. Co-design does not\n999\trequire end-to-end training. It first requires a shared objective and matched evaluation.\n1000\tNaive end-to-end training is not sufficient. As the encoder changes, the generator sees a moving target.\n1001\tAs predictability is rewarded, the encoder may discard difficult but perceptually important information.\n1002\tUseful approaches must constrain distortion and still improve the empirical profile in Equation 2, for\n1003\texample through alternating updates, stop-gradient paths, or a slowly moving tokenizer. The decisive\n1004\tbaseline is a frozen codec with the same generator compute and perceptual quality.\n1005\t\n1006\t7.3\n1007\t\n1008\tDesign the latent and model jointly for streaming\n1009\t\n1010\tStreaming is not an encoder property alone. It jointly constrains frame boundaries, latent state, modeling\n1011\tstrategy, and sampling schedule. Audio’s information density is non-stationary, so silence, sustained\n1012\tvowels, plosives, and music onsets need not receive the same rate. TADA [59] and FlexiCodec [60]\n1013\texplore variable-rate representations, but removing the regular clock also changes positional encoding,\n1014\tbuffering, and the downstream prediction problem.\n1015\tA complete streaming design must specify the boundary policy, the information carried at each\n1016\tboundary, and the model that advances that state under bounded lookahead. For continuous latents, the\n1017\tcorresponding experiment is a semantic-rate curve: compress the same causal SSL representation across\n1018\tdimensions and frame rates, then measure understanding, reconstruction, continuation, and real-time cost\n1019\twith matched downstream models. This would show whether a single continuous stream is sufficient or\n1020\twhether a small structured set of streams is needed.\n1021\t\n1022\t7.4\n1023\t\n1024\tDecide what requires long-context modeling\n1025\t\n1026\tThe semantic-acoustic split should not be fixed by convention. Different tasks have different long-range\n1027\tvariables. Linguistic content dominates speech continuation. Melody, rhythm, instrumentation, speaker\n1028\tidentity, room acoustics, or expressive prosody may instead remain global in other settings. The research\n1029\tquestion is which variables must be exposed before local acoustic generation, and where that boundary\n1030\tshould appear: between tokenizers, between RVQ layers, between model stages (Section 4.2), or between\n1031\tthe outer and inner loops of a hybrid model (Section 6.3).\n1032\tCompatibility with text LLMs is one instance of this problem. Discrete tokens fit a softmax interface\n1033\tbut can dominate context length and need not align with text semantics. Continuous features may align\n1034\tmore naturally with hidden states but require projectors and a generative output head. A meaningful\n1035\tcomparison should test understanding and generation in the same backbone under a fixed wall-clock\n1036\tbudget. Interface convenience should not be mistaken for a well-placed semantic boundary.\n1037\t\n1038\t17\n1039\t\n1040\t\f8\n1041\t\n1042\tConclusion\n1043\t\n1044\tThe distinction between discrete and continuous latents describes the interface, but it is not sufficient\n1045\tto explain an audio generation system. The representation determines where rate, information, and\n1046\tuncertainty are placed. The modeling strategy determines which dependencies receive long-context\n1047\tprediction and how the remaining ambiguity is represented. These choices must be evaluated together\n1048\tunder constraints on distortion, compute, and streaming.\n1049\tThe view that semantics is low-entropy and acoustics is high-entropy provides a useful starting\n1050\tintuition, but the more general diagnostic questions concern dependency horizon and conditional ambiguity. AudioLM [7] exposes a semantic trajectory before acoustic realization. RVQ creates an ordered\n1051\tcapacity axis without guaranteeing semantic order. How much of the remaining realization ambiguity a\n1052\tsystem can then represent depends on its modeling scheme, whether that is RVQ’s chain rule, a Gaussian\n1053\tmixture, or a diffusion process, not on whether the latent is discrete or continuous. Continuous and\n1054\thybrid systems place these same divisions at different locations. The four-objective design space and\n1055\tworked comparisons provide a common language for comparing these representation–model pairs and\n1056\tfor deciding which part of the pair future work should change.\n1057\t\n1058\tReferences\n1059\t[1] Zeghidour et al. SoundStream: An End-to-End Neural Audio Codec. https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.\n1060\t03312, 2021. IEEE/ACM TASLP.\n1061\t[2] Défossez et al. High Fidelity Neural Audio Compression. https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.13438, 2023.\n1062\t(EnCodec), TMLR.\n1063\t[3] Wang et al. Neural Codec Language Models are Zero-Shot Text to Speech Synthesizers. https://arxiv.\n1064\torg/abs/2301.02111, 2023. (VALL-E).\n1065\t[4] Copet et al. Simple and Controllable Music Generation. https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.05284, 2023.\n1066\t(MusicGen), NeurIPS.\n1067\t[5] Yang et al. UniAudio: An Audio Foundation Model Toward Universal Audio Generation. https://arxiv.\n1068\torg/abs/2310.00704, 2024. ICML.\n1069\t[6] Défossez et al. Moshi: a speech-text foundation model for real-time dialogue. https://arxiv.org/abs/\n1070\t2410.00037, 2024. (incl. Mimi).\n1071\t[7] Borsos et al. AudioLM: a Language Modeling Approach to Audio Generation. https://arxiv.org/abs/\n1072\t2209.03143, 2022.\n1073\t[8] Jia et al. DiTAR: Diffusion Transformer Autoregressive Modeling for Speech Generation. https://arxiv.\n1074\torg/abs/2502.03930, 2025. ICML.\n1075\t[9] Simon Rouard, Manu Orsini, Axel Roebel, Neil Zeghidour, and Alexandre Défossez. Continuous Audio\n1076\tLanguage Models. https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.06926, 2025. (CALM; consistency head over an audio\n1077\tVAE; from the Mimi/Moshi team).\n1078\t[10] Thomas M. Cover and Joy A. Thomas. Elements of Information Theory. Wiley-Interscience, 2nd edition, 2006.\n1079\t[11] Yang et al. UniAudio 1.5: LLM-driven Audio Codec is A Few-shot Audio Task Learner. https://arxiv.\n1080\torg/abs/2406.10056, 2024. (LLM-Codec), NeurIPS.\n1081\t[12] Tishby, Pereira, and Bialek. The Information Bottleneck Method. https://arxiv.org/abs/physics/\n1082\t0004057, 2000.\n1083\t\n1084\t18\n1085\t\n1086\t\f[13] Kumar et al. High-Fidelity Audio Compression with Improved RVQGAN. https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.\n1087\t06546, 2023. (DAC), NeurIPS.\n1088\t[14] Mousavi et al. Discrete Audio Tokens: More Than a Survey! https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.10274, 2025.\n1089\tTMLR.\n1090\t[15] Yang et al. HiFi-Codec: Group-residual Vector quantization for High Fidelity Audio Codec. https://arxiv.\n1091\torg/abs/2305.02765, 2023.\n1092\t[16] Ji et al. WavTokenizer: an Efficient Acoustic Discrete Codec Tokenizer for Audio Language Modeling.\n1093\thttps://arxiv.org/abs/2408.16532, 2025. ICLR.\n1094\t[17] Xin et al. BigCodec: Pushing the Limits of Low-Bitrate Neural Speech Codec. https://arxiv.org/abs/\n1095\t2409.05377, 2024.\n1096\t[18] Mentzer et al. Finite Scalar Quantization: VQ-VAE Made Simple. https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.15505,\n1097\t2024. (FSQ), ICLR.\n1098\t[19] Borsos et al. S